---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import talk from '../assets/talk.jpg';
import { paper } from '../data/resume';
---

<section id="research" class="section research grid-12" aria-labelledby="research-title">
  <figure class="research__photo">
    <Image
      class="photo"
      src={talk}
      widths={[480, 926]}
      sizes="(max-width: 900px) calc(100vw - 40px), 45vw"
      alt="Gerald presenting a talk titled Breaking Bad Symmetries, the title set in Breaking Bad's periodic-table style"
    />
    <figcaption class="meta">
      Giving the talk at the Pacific Northwest Section meeting of the Mathematical Association of America, Oregon State
      University, April 2016. It's talk 87 in the <a href={paper.programUrl}>program</a>.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="research__text body-copy">
    <h2 id="research-title">Breaking bad symmetries</h2>
    <p>
      My master's research, in plain terms. Draw a picture made of points and lines. A symmetry shuffles the points
      without breaking any of the lines. The fixing number is how many points you have to pin down before the only
      symmetry left is doing nothing at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      I worked on the Dowling geometry, a picture that encodes a group's multiplication table. I counted every one of its
      symmetries and found its fixing number.
    </p>
    <div class="theorem math">
      <p>
        <strong>Theorem</strong> <em>(Todd, 2016).</em> Let <em>G</em> be a finite group of order <em>n</em>, and
        <em>Q<sub>r</sub></em>(<em>G</em>) its rank-<em>r</em> Dowling geometry, <em>r</em> ≥ 3. Then
      </p>
      <p class="theorem__result">
        <span><em>|</em>Aut <em>Q<sub>r</sub></em>(<em>G</em>)<em>|</em> = <em>r</em>! <em>n</em>! <em>n</em><sup><em>r</em>−2</sup></span>
        <span>fix <em>Q<sub>r</sub></em>(<em>G</em>) = <em>n</em> + <em>r</em> − 3</span>
      </p>
      <p class="theorem__note">The two main results of my paper, proved there in full.</p>
    </div>
    <p>
      The surprise is what's missing. Neither answer depends on how the group is built, only on its size
      <em class="math">n</em>.
    </p>
    <p>
      My adviser was {paper.adviser}, who co-wrote the textbook on matroids. She's now the dean of arts and sciences at
      the University of Alaska Anchorage, where I did my undergrad.
      <a href={paper.url}>Read the paper (PDF)</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .research {
    row-gap: 40px;
    align-items: start;
    border-top-color: var(--ink);
  }

  .research__photo {
    grid-column: span 6;
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 14px;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .research__photo :global(.photo) {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border-radius: 6px;
  }

  .research__text {
    grid-column: 8 / span 5;
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: clamp(34px, 4vw, 44px);
    line-height: 1.05;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .theorem {
    padding: 20px 24px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--accent);
    background: var(--paper-deep);
    color: var(--ink);
    font-size: 18px;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .theorem strong {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .theorem__result {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    margin: 10px 0;
    font-size: clamp(20px, 2.4vw, 24px);
  }

  .theorem__note {
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 13.5px;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  sup {
    line-height: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .research__photo,
    .research__text {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
    }
  }
</style>
